<p>At a Brussels summit on March 19, European Union leaders failed to win Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orban’s approval for a €90 billion loan package for Ukraine, leaving the measure blocked by Hungary for now. European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen said early on March 20 that the bloc would still find ways to get the money to Kyiv despite Hungary’s opposition, signaling that support for Ukraine remains politically strong even as the mechanism is contested. The dispute centers on Hungary’s demand that Russian oil deliveries through the Druzhba pipeline be restored before it backs the loan, with Slovakia also linking its position to the oil issue according to reports from the summit. Several leaders reacted angrily, and some accused Orban of reversing course on a loan that had been agreed in principle in December, as Hungary heads into an April 12 election in which his stance on Ukraine has become a campaign issue.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/KmkExlLI-Bv7NuvDlGucpJ3Ndg1fWVsNWQPbQm5w-yQl6CaNm4kt6gOWjMNsxDmwx7Y7kDpP-d_1E1wW-4iGZq9vnpPRwi7PGLbtsx95o-Pgd-FMNkEXVXuuLm73QS4vAaauNIm9D0MA3Jti-dJMo7v23AG-WFbrpXCNycwroV3bAtFe8fiDiwQr9JO9-aeBa88E1V1NGcM' alt='Prime Minister Viktor Orban of Hungary in Budapest on Sunday. His disputes with President Volodymyr Zelensky of Ukraine have become a focal point of Hungary’s coming election.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Sharp criticism: German Chancellor Friedrich Merz called Orban’s reversal “a gross act of disloyalty,” making the dispute notable for how openly leaders described the damage to trust inside the EU.</li><li>February trigger: RT reported that Budapest did not turn against the package until February, after Ukraine halted Russian oil flows, linking the blockage to a later deterioration in the transit dispute.</li><li>Infrastructure spillover: SCMP reported that Orban has pointed to a dispute over a war-damaged pipeline, showing how physical energy infrastructure has fed directly into the argument over financing Ukraine.</li></ul><blockquote>I am firmly convinced that it will leave deep marks. - Friedrich Merz</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>European Commission: Ursula von der Leyen emphasized continuity, signaling that the Commission’s priority is to keep funding flowing to Kyiv even if the original approval route remains blocked. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/eu-will-find-ways-to-get-loan-to-ukraine-von-der-leyen-says'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Hungary: Budapest is treating the loan as leverage in its dispute with Ukraine over interrupted oil transit, tying an EU financial measure to an energy grievance. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/635533-eu-hungary-ukraine-loan/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Germany: Friedrich Merz framed Orban’s move as a breach of trust, making Berlin’s complaint as much about EU credibility as about Ukraine aid. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/19/hungary-orban-ukraine-loan-veto-eu-summit-europe'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Slovakia: Bratislava’s stance suggests the quarrel is not just about Orban, but about a broader Central European sensitivity to pipeline disruptions. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/eu-summit-hungary-holds-ukraine-aid-ransom-over-druzhba-oil/live-76430675'>DW</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/video/2026/03/19/belgium-eu-leaders-meet-as-ukraine-loan-remains-blocked'>Belgium: EU leaders meet as Ukraine loan remains blocked</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/eu-will-find-ways-to-get-loan-to-ukraine-von-der-leyen-says'>EU will find ways to get loan to Ukraine, von der Leyen says</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/19/world/europe/ukraine-hungary-orban-elections.html'>A Crucial Loan for Ukraine Becomes Election Leverage for Orban</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/19/hungary-orban-ukraine-loan-veto-eu-summit-eur